  "In 1947 there were three partitions: of British India, and of the provinces of Bengal and Punjab. [[c-part-three]] India's Constituent Assembly met at midnight of 15 August, but the boundary awards drawn up by Sir Cyril Radcliffe were published only on 17 August. [[c-part-radcliffe]] Communal violence followed. Most estimates put the dead at around one million, but the figure 'continues to be a contentious issue', and estimates range from 200,000 to 2 million. [[c-part-deaths]] About 14 to 14.5 million people crossed the new borders. [[c-part-moved]]",
  "In Palestine (for the Mandate, see 15.3), the UN General Assembly voted on 29 November 1947, by 33 to 13 with 10 abstentions, for a plan to end the Mandate and divide the country into an Arab and a Jewish state, with Jerusalem internationalized. [[c-part-181]] On 14 May 1948 David Ben-Gurion proclaimed the State of Israel, and President Truman recognized it the same day. [[c-part-israel]] In 1948 over half of the Palestinian Arab population fled or were expelled. The traditional Israeli account held that they were urged to flee by their own leaders, the Arab account that they were driven out by the Jews. Benny Morris, working from Israeli military archives, counts 700,000 refugees and concluded in his first study (1987) that the problem 'was born of war, not by design, Jewish or Arab'. Ilan Pappé writes of 800,000 expelled and argues that 'ethnic cleansing was not a circumstance of war, but rather a deliberate goal of combat'. [[c-part-exodus]]",
