  "At Yalta in February 1945, Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin agreed a declaration on Poland that brought Communists into its postwar government, and promised the Soviet Union a sphere of influence in Manchuria in return for joining the war against Japan. [[c-cw-yalta]] In March 1947 President Truman asked Congress for $400 million for Greece and Turkey, declaring it US policy 'to support free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation by armed minorities or by outside pressures'. [[c-cw-truman]] That June, Marshall called for a programme to rebuild Europe; US aid rose to over $12 billion, while 'Stalin's unwillingness to open up his secret society to westerners' helped doom Soviet participation. [[c-cw-marshall]] The North Atlantic Treaty of April 1949 declared that 'an armed attack against one or more of them in Europe or North America shall be considered an attack against them all'; after West Germany joined NATO in 1955, the Soviet Union and seven eastern European states formed the Warsaw Pact. [[c-cw-nato]]",
